<!--
    GH-9230: "Expand builtin function recovery to memset / bzero"
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/9230

    Source (foo1, clang-18 -O3):
        void foo1() { unsigned char foo[100]; bzero(foo,100); printf(foo); }
    Compiled: inlined bzero = 6x movaps xmm0(=0),N(rsp) + 1x movl 0,0x60(rsp).

    Default output: the 100-byte zero fill is emitted as ~88 individual char-array
    element stores acStack_68[0xNN] = NUL instead of a single builtin_memset.
    The existing constseq recovery (RuleStringCopy/StringSequence, S5 named group
    constsequence) collapses constant CHARACTER copies into builtin_strncpy, but
    ArraySequence::formByteArray treats the first zero byte as a null terminator,
    so an all-zero fill counts as length 1 (less than MINIMUM_SEQUENCE_LENGTH=4)
    and the sequence is rejected.

    Stage model: S5 simplification, named group constsequence. A new constant-fill
    sub-stage (memsetsequence) detects a single repeated fill byte over the
    contiguous region and emits builtin_memset(dst,val,len). Enabled via a P0
    option (default off = upstream element stores), so assertion 1 reproduces
    under the default pipeline.

    Pass 1 (default): assertion 1 proves the bug reproduces (element stores).
    Pass 2 (option memsetsequence on): assertion 2 asserts the fix emits a single
    builtin_memset call covering the whole 100-byte region.

    Note: parse line extern void printf(char *) is load-bearing; it types the rsp
    buffer as char acStack_68[104], yielding the issue's indexed-store form. The
    printf stub at 0x1000f0 is the target of the relative call in foo1's bytes.
-->
